== Module (chicken continuation)

This module provides a more powerful interface for continuations than that
provided by {{call/cc}}.

More information about this continuation API can be found in the paper
[[http://www.iro.umontreal.ca/~feeley/papers/FeeleySW01.pdf|A Better
API for First-Class Continuations]] by Marc Feeley.

=== Continuations API

==== continuation-capture

<procedure>(continuation-capture PROCEDURE)</procedure>

Creates a continuation object representing the current continuation and
tail-calls {{PROCEDURE}} with this continuation as the single argument.


==== continuation?

<procedure>(continuation? X)</procedure>

Returns {{#t}} if {{X}} is a continuation object, or {{#f}} otherwise. Please
note that this applies only to continuations created by the Continuation API,
but not by call/cc, i.e.: {{(call-with-current-continuation continuation?)}}
returns {{#f}}, whereas {{(continuation-capture continuation?)}} returns
{{#t}}.


==== continuation-graft

<procedure>(continuation-graft CONT THUNK)</procedure>

Calls the procedure {{THUNK}} with no arguments and the implicit continuation
{{CONT}}.


==== continuation-return

<procedure>(continuation-return CONT VALUE ...)</procedure>

Returns the value(s) to the continuation {{CONT}}. {{continuation-return}} could
be implemented like this:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define (continuation-return k . vals)
  (continuation-graft
    k
    (lambda () (apply values vals))))
</enscript>


=== Delimited continuations

{{shift}} and {{reset}} are Danvy and Filinski's delimited control
operators, described in
[[https://doi.org/10.1145/91556.91622|Abstracting Control]].
Unlike {{call/cc}}, which captures the whole rest of the computation,
{{shift}} captures only the part of it that is enclosed by a {{reset}},
and hands it over as an ordinary procedure that ''returns'' a value to
its caller, so that it composes.

==== reset

<macro>(reset BODY ...)</macro>

Evaluates {{BODY}} with a delimiter installed around it. The value of the
{{reset}} expression is the value of {{BODY}}, unless a {{shift}} in its
dynamic extent returns some other value to the delimiter.

==== shift

<macro>(shift VARIABLE BODY ...)</macro>

Captures the continuation of the {{shift}} expression up to, but not
including, the nearest enclosing {{reset}}, binds {{VARIABLE}} to it as a
one-argument procedure, and evaluates {{BODY}} with the {{reset}} itself
as its continuation. Calling the captured procedure runs the captured
piece of computation and ''returns'' its value, so it can be called zero,
one or many times, and its results composed:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(+ 1 (reset (+ 2 (shift f (f (f 3))))))    ; => 8
(reset (+ 1 (shift f 10)))                 ; => 10
(reset (begin (shift k (cons 1 (k #f)))
              (shift k (cons 2 (k #f)))
              '()))                        ; => (1 2)
</enscript>

The delimiter is the innermost {{reset}} on the ''dynamic'' extent, not
the lexically enclosing one, so a procedure containing a {{shift}} is
delimited by whatever {{reset}} its caller has installed. It is an error
to evaluate a {{shift}} with no enclosing {{reset}}.

Leaving a delimited segment runs the {{after}} thunks of any
{{dynamic-wind}} between the {{shift}} and its delimiter, and resuming
one runs the corresponding {{before}} thunks again, so a segment that is
resumed twice is wound twice.

Two things are worth knowing about the implementation. First, the
compiler recognises {{shift}} and {{reset}} and compiles them directly
through its CPS conversion, which is where the continuation they need
already exists; capturing it costs nothing at run time. Interpreted code
uses equivalent library procedures and interoperates with compiled code.
Second, a delimiter passes exactly one value, so {{(reset (values 1 2))}}
yields {{1}}.

